1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,383
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,383 --> 00:00:08,543
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:08,543 --> 00:00:18,355
UnitedHealth Group Incorporated Common Stock (DE), ticker UNH triggered, then faded to -1.4 percent below the entry.

4
00:00:18,355 --> 00:00:23,261
If it closes a week below the stop, the exit is automatic.

5
00:00:23,261 --> 00:00:26,645
That is not a failure; that is the plan.

6
00:00:26,645 --> 00:00:31,581
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

7
00:00:31,581 --> 00:00:36,989
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

8
00:00:36,954 --> 00:00:41,094
On the day, the book is up 483 dollars.

9
00:00:41,094 --> 00:00:46,401
Diamondrock Hospitality (DRH) leads, +3.1 percent.

10
00:00:46,401 --> 00:00:51,284
Philip Morris International (PM) lags at -2.8.

11
00:00:51,284 --> 00:00:57,016
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

12
00:00:57,016 --> 00:01:00,543
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

13
00:01:00,499 --> 00:01:08,277
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

14
00:01:08,277 --> 00:01:15,890
11 positions, led by MetLife, ticker MET, Microsoft, ticker MSFT, Eli Lilly and, ticker LLY.

15
00:01:15,890 --> 00:01:27,144
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

16
00:01:27,109 --> 00:01:31,978
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

17
00:01:31,978 --> 00:01:34,412
Every morning OROS proposes.

18
00:01:34,412 --> 00:01:45,106
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

19
00:01:45,106 --> 00:01:49,106
Inside the book today: 6 positions up, 5 down.

20
00:01:49,106 --> 00:01:51,540
Best, AVPT at +6.21 percent.

21
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:53,192
Worst, PM at -2.76.

22
00:01:53,192 --> 00:01:58,235
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

23
00:01:58,235 --> 00:02:05,538
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -3.23% per trade over 1166 graded positions.

24
00:02:05,538 --> 00:02:12,667
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

25
00:02:12,667 --> 00:02:17,884
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

26
00:02:17,884 --> 00:02:30,404
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 165 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

27
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:32,613
Over to the sports desk.

28
00:02:32,613 --> 00:02:41,905
Day 74 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

29
00:02:41,905 --> 00:02:48,194
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,028.

30
00:02:48,194 --> 00:02:53,732
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.0 thousand back at 102,985.

31
00:02:53,732 --> 00:03:00,960
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 7.1 thousand back at 96,915.

32
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:03,588
The race has no finish line.

33
00:03:03,588 --> 00:03:07,905
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

34
00:03:07,905 --> 00:03:12,786
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

35
00:03:12,786 --> 00:03:14,664
Nothing is restated.

36
00:03:14,664 --> 00:03:16,993
Today's lesson is benchmarking.

37
00:03:16,993 --> 00:03:23,455
Any strategy must beat the boring alternative: the same money parked in an index fund.

38
00:03:23,455 --> 00:03:26,911
That is why the S and P line shares the chart.

39
00:03:26,911 --> 00:03:35,927
If the system cannot outrun it, the index deserves the money, and publishing the race every day keeps the system honest.

40
00:03:35,886 --> 00:03:43,221
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

41
00:03:43,221 --> 00:03:45,462
Nothing here is financial advice.

42
00:03:45,462 --> 00:03:48,451
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
